      {type:"Long",q:"31. Explain the chemical properties of acids with equations.",a:"Acids react with metals to form salt and hydrogen: Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂. They react with metal carbonates to form salt, water and carbon dioxide: 2HCl + Na₂CO₃ → 2NaCl + H₂O + CO₂. They neutralise bases: HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O. Acidic solutions conduct electricity because they contain ions."},
      {type:"Long",q:"32. Explain the pH scale and give its importance in everyday life.",a:"The pH scale indicates how acidic or basic a solution is. At 25°C, pH 7 is neutral, below 7 is acidic and above 7 is basic. pH is important in digestion, tooth protection, soil treatment and the survival of aquatic organisms. The pH scale is logarithmic, so a one-unit change represents a tenfold change in hydrogen-ion concentration."},
      {type:"Long",q:"33. Describe the preparation and uses of baking soda and washing soda.",a:"Baking soda is sodium hydrogen carbonate, NaHCO₃. It is used in baking, as a mild antacid and in some fire extinguishers. Washing soda is sodium carbonate decahydrate, Na₂CO₃·10H₂O. It is used as a cleaning agent and to remove permanent hardness of water."},
      {type:"Long",q:"34. Explain bleaching powder and plaster of Paris, including their uses.",a:"Bleaching powder is represented at this level by CaOCl₂ and is used for bleaching and disinfecting water. Plaster of Paris, CaSO₄·½H₂O, is made by heating gypsum. It is used for casts, moulds and decorative work. When mixed with water, it sets into a hard mass."},
      {type:"Long",q:"35. Why is dilution of concentrated acid an exothermic process, and what is the safe method?",a:"Mixing concentrated acid with water releases heat. Adding water rapidly to concentrated acid may cause local boiling and splashing. The safer method is to add acid slowly to water with stirring, following proper laboratory safety precautions."}
