      {type:"very-short",q:"20. What is evolution?",a:"Heritable changes in populations over generations."},
      {type:"short",q:"21. Why do offspring resemble their parents?",a:"They inherit genes from their parents, which influence their characteristics."},
      {type:"short",q:"22. Why is variation important?",a:"Variation may help some individuals survive environmental changes and reproduce."},
      {type:"short",q:"23. Why did Mendel choose pea plants?",a:"They have clear contrasting traits, a short generation time and can be cross-pollinated under control."},
      {type:"short",q:"24. Explain dominant and recessive traits.",a:"If T represents dominant tallness and t represents dwarfness, TT and Tt are tall while tt is dwarf."},
      {type:"short",q:"25. Differentiate genotype and phenotype.",a:"Genotype is the genetic combination, such as Tt. Phenotype is the observable trait, such as tallness."},
      {type:"short",q:"26. What happens in TT × tt?",a:"All F1 offspring are Tt and show the dominant trait."},
      {type:"short",q:"27. How does sexual reproduction create variation?",a:"It combines genetic material from two parents and produces new gene combinations."},
      {type:"short",q:"28. Why are acquired traits generally not inherited?",a:"They usually do not change DNA in reproductive cells."},
      {type:"short",q:"29. How is chromosomal sex determined in humans?",a:"The egg usually contributes X; the sperm contributes X or Y, typically resulting in XX or XY."},
      {type:"short",q:"30. How are heredity and evolution related?",a:"Heredity passes genetic information, while inherited variations can accumulate over generations."},
      {type:"long",q:"31. Explain Mendel's monohybrid cross.",a:"Mendel crossed pure tall TT and dwarf tt pea plants. All F1 offspring were Tt and tall. Self-pollination of F1 plants (Tt × Tt) produced TT, Tt, Tt and tt. The expected phenotypic ratio is 3 tall : 1 dwarf, and the genotypic ratio is 1:2:1."},
      {type:"long",q:"32. Explain how traits are inherited.",a:"Genes are sections of DNA on chromosomes. Parents pass genetic material to offspring through gametes. In sexual reproduction, each parent generally contributes one set of chromosomes. The combination of inherited alleles influences traits, and environmental factors may also affect their expression."},
      {type:"long",q:"33. Differentiate inherited and acquired traits.",a:"Inherited traits are influenced by genetic information passed from parents, such as blood group. Acquired traits develop during life, such as learning handwriting. Acquired traits generally do not change the hereditary DNA in reproductive cells and therefore are usually not inherited."},
      {type:"long",q:"34. Explain sex determination in humans.",a:"Typically, females have XX and males have XY sex chromosomes. Eggs carry X, while sperm carry either X or Y. An X-bearing sperm usually results in XX offspring, and a Y-bearing sperm usually results in XY offspring. These are typical chromosomal patterns."},
      {type:"long",q:"35. Explain the role of variation in evolution.",a:"Variations arise through DNA changes and new gene combinations. Some inherited variations may help organisms survive and reproduce. Natural selection can increase the frequency of advantageous variations over generations, contributing to evolution."}
