      {type:"very-short",q:"18. Write the formula for electric power.",a:"P = VI."},
      {type:"very-short",q:"19. What is the SI unit of electric power?",a:"Watt (W)."},
      {type:"very-short",q:"20. What is one kilowatt-hour?",a:"The energy consumed by a 1 kW device operating for one hour; 3.6 × 10⁶ J."},

      {type:"short",q:"21. Define one ampere.",a:"One ampere is the current when one coulomb of charge flows through a cross-section in one second."},
      {type:"short",q:"22. Explain potential difference.",a:"Potential difference between two points is the work done per unit charge to move a charge between them. V = W/Q."},
      {type:"short",q:"23. State two factors affecting resistance.",a:"Resistance increases with wire length and decreases with cross-sectional area, for the same material and temperature."},
      {type:"short",q:"24. Why are household appliances connected in parallel?",a:"Each appliance receives the same supply voltage and can be switched independently. A fault in one branch need not stop all other branches."},
      {type:"short",q:"25. Compare series and parallel combinations.",a:"In series, the same current passes through each resistor and equivalent resistance is the sum. In parallel, the same potential difference acts across each branch and the reciprocal resistances add."},
      {type:"short",q:"26. Calculate current when V = 12 V and R = 4 Ω.",a:"Using I = V/R, I = 12/4 = 3 A."},
      {type:"short",q:"27. Calculate resistance when V = 6 V and I = 0.5 A.",a:"R = V/I = 6/0.5 = 12 Ω."},
      {type:"short",q:"28. What is the heating effect of electric current?",a:"When current passes through a resistance, electrical energy is converted into heat. For constant resistance, H = I²Rt."},
      {type:"short",q:"29. State Joule's law of heating.",a:"Heat produced is proportional to the square of current, resistance and time: H = I²Rt."},
      {type:"short",q:"30. A device uses 100 W for 2 hours. Find energy in kWh.",a:"Energy = 0.1 kW × 2 h = 0.2 kWh."},

      {type:"long",q:"31. Explain Ohm's law and describe its mathematical form.",a:"Ohm's law states that at constant temperature and other physical conditions, current through a conductor is directly proportional to the potential difference across it. Thus V ∝ I and V = IR, where R is resistance. A graph of V against I is a straight line through the origin for an ohmic conductor under these conditions."},
      {type:"long",q:"32. Derive the equivalent resistance of resistors connected in series.",a:"In series, the same current I flows through each resistor. The total potential difference is V = V₁ + V₂ + V₃. Using Ohm's law, V = IR, V₁ = IR₁, V₂ = IR₂ and V₃ = IR₃. Therefore IR = IR₁ + IR₂ + IR₃, giving R = R₁ + R₂ + R₃."},
      {type:"long",q:"33. Derive the equivalent resistance of resistors connected in parallel.",a:"In parallel, each resistor has the same potential difference V. The total current is I = I₁ + I₂ + I₃. Using Ohm's law, I = V/R and each branch current is V/R₁, V/R₂ and V/R₃. Thus V/R = V/R₁ + V/R₂ + V/R₃. Dividing by V gives 1/R = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + 1/R₃."},
      {type:"long",q:"34. Explain the heating effect of electric current and give examples.",a:"When current passes through a resistor, collisions between charge carriers and the material transfer electrical energy into heat. Joule's law gives H = I²Rt. This effect is used in electric heaters, irons and toasters. A fuse also uses heating to melt and break a circuit when excessive current flows."},
      {type:"long",q:"35. Define electric power and calculate the power of a device operating at 220 V and 2 A.",a:"Electric power is the rate at which electrical energy is consumed or converted. P = VI. Therefore P = 220 × 2 = 440 W. Power can also be written as P = I²R or P = V²/R for an ohmic resistor."}
